1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Reading a Query Plan. SQL describes the answer. The plan describes the work.

2
00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,600
Your SQL says WHAT you want: the 20 newest open tickets.

3
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:12,600
It never says HOW to find them. Choosing the how is the planner's job.

4
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:14,600
EXPLAIN shows the plan: a tree. Each child feeds rows UP to its parent.

5
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:20,900
Read it inside out: start at the deepest node.

6
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:25,200
Seq Scan reads all 100,000 rows: keeps 2,000 open ones, removes 98,000.

7
00:00:25,700 --> 00:00:28,200
Sort receives 2,000 candidates and compares them to find the newest.

8
00:00:28,700 --> 00:00:30,900
Limit stops after 20. Only 20 rows come out of the top...

9
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:34,400
...but 100,000 were examined to get them. "rows=20" at the top is not "20 rows of work".

10
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:42,500
Compare estimated rows with actual rows: a big gap means the planner guessed wrong.

11
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,000
cost=... is in planner units, NOT milliseconds.

12
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:55,400
Remember: SQL = the answer you want.  Plan = the work to get it. Read plans inside out: the deepest node runs first. Rows flow up; the top count hides the work below. EXPLAIN estimates.  EXPLAIN ANALYZE really runs it.

